WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.527
Most civilian cloud systems
are authorized at Moderate,

2
00:00:03.527 --> 00:00:05.450
and that word is doing real work.

3
00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.190
The agency rates the impact
of losing confidentiality,

4
00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:13.012
integrity and availability,
and the highest rating wins.

5
00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:17.412
If nothing is high and at least one thing
is moderate, it's a Moderate system.

6
00:00:17.412 --> 00:00:20.482
The agency then tailors
NIST's Moderate baseline,

7
00:00:20.482 --> 00:00:23.551
which is the size of the
promise that system makes.

8
00:00:23.551 --> 00:00:26.287
FedRAMP's Class C is a separate fact.

9
00:00:26.287 --> 00:00:30.278
It includes the Moderate baseline,
but it lists more controls,

10
00:00:30.278 --> 00:00:33.251
a few dozen of them outside
NIST's Moderate set.

11
00:00:33.251 --> 00:00:34.988
So cite the two separately.

12
00:00:34.988 --> 00:00:38.181
A Moderate system built on
a Class C service inherits

13
00:00:38.181 --> 00:00:41.012
the provider's evidence
for the provider's side.

14
00:00:41.012 --> 00:00:45.451
It still has to prove its own side,
against its own tailored baseline.

15
00:00:45.451 --> 00:00:49.440
Which raises the real question:
when is a control actually closed?
